1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,985
Before the sun comes up,

2
00:00:01,985 --> 00:00:03,308
a rooster crows.

3
00:00:03,488 --> 00:00:05,528
A light clicks on in the farmhouse

4
00:00:05,528 --> 00:00:06,008
kitchen.

5
00:00:06,188 --> 00:00:08,253
A man pulls on his boots by

6
00:00:08,253 --> 00:00:09,324
the back door,

7
00:00:09,324 --> 00:00:11,772
while his wife lights the stove.

8
00:00:11,952 --> 00:00:12,978
Out in the barn,

9
00:00:12,978 --> 00:00:15,156
the cows are waiting to be milked.

10
00:00:15,336 --> 00:00:17,465
It's four-thirty in the morning,

11
00:00:17,465 --> 00:00:19,927
in the spring of nineteen fifty-five,

12
00:00:19,927 --> 00:00:21,989
and on an American family farm,

13
00:00:21,989 --> 00:00:23,719
the day has already begun.

14
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:25,685
In nineteen fifty-five,

15
00:00:25,685 --> 00:00:28,636
there were still nearly five million farms

16
00:00:28,636 --> 00:00:30,112
in the United States,

17
00:00:30,112 --> 00:00:32,853
and around one in eight Americans lived

18
00:00:32,853 --> 00:00:33,345
on one.

19
00:00:33,525 --> 00:00:35,098
Most were family farms,

20
00:00:35,098 --> 00:00:37,767
small enough that one family could work

21
00:00:37,767 --> 00:00:40,298
them, with help from the neighbors at

22
00:00:40,298 --> 00:00:41,187
harvest time.

23
00:00:41,367 --> 00:00:44,211
Today, there are fewer than two million

24
00:00:44,211 --> 00:00:46,398
farms, and only about one in a

25
00:00:46,398 --> 00:00:48,659
hundred Americans lives on one.

26
00:00:49,008 --> 00:00:51,137
So let's spend a year on an

27
00:00:51,137 --> 00:00:54,604
American family farm in nineteen fifty-five.

28
00:00:54,784 --> 00:00:56,154
Season by season.

29
00:00:56,334 --> 00:00:57,636
The work, the food,

30
00:00:57,636 --> 00:00:59,966
the community, and the way of life

31
00:00:59,966 --> 00:01:02,639
that has almost completely disappeared.

32
00:01:02,819 --> 00:01:04,319
If you grew up on a farm,

33
00:01:04,319 --> 00:01:06,421
I think you'll smell the hay before

34
00:01:06,421 --> 00:01:07,741
we're halfway through.

35
00:01:08,091 --> 00:01:09,860
Spring was planting season,

36
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:11,759
and it was a race against the

37
00:01:11,759 --> 00:01:12,283
weather.

38
00:01:12,463 --> 00:01:13,965
Fields had to be plowed,

39
00:01:13,965 --> 00:01:16,031
disked and planted as soon as the

40
00:01:16,031 --> 00:01:17,095
ground dried out.

41
00:01:17,275 --> 00:01:19,054
In nineteen fifty-five,

42
00:01:19,054 --> 00:01:21,606
most farms finally had a tractor.

43
00:01:21,786 --> 00:01:23,150
A red Farmall,

44
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:25,002
a green John Deere,

45
00:01:25,002 --> 00:01:26,464
or a gray Ford.

46
00:01:26,644 --> 00:01:28,381
Just a generation earlier,

47
00:01:28,381 --> 00:01:30,452
most of this work had been done

48
00:01:30,452 --> 00:01:31,254
with horses.

49
00:01:31,603 --> 00:01:33,322
Those tractors were simple.

50
00:01:33,502 --> 00:01:36,243
No cab, no air conditioning,

51
00:01:36,243 --> 00:01:37,124
no radio.

52
00:01:37,304 --> 00:01:39,649
The farmer sat on a metal seat

53
00:01:39,649 --> 00:01:40,587
in the wind,

54
00:01:40,587 --> 00:01:41,604
dust and sun,

55
00:01:41,604 --> 00:01:44,340
for twelve or fourteen hours a day.

56
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:46,874
And many older farmers still kept a

57
00:01:46,874 --> 00:01:48,893
team of horses for small jobs,

58
00:01:48,893 --> 00:01:51,382
or just because they couldn't bear to

59
00:01:51,382 --> 00:01:52,391
part with them.

60
00:01:52,741 --> 00:01:55,871
A typical Midwest family farm in nineteen

61
00:01:55,871 --> 00:01:58,848
fifty-five grew a little of everything.

62
00:01:59,028 --> 00:02:02,256
Corn, oats, hay and soybeans.

63
00:02:02,436 --> 00:02:04,109
There were dairy cows,

64
00:02:04,109 --> 00:02:05,478
hogs and chickens.

65
00:02:05,658 --> 00:02:07,724
A big vegetable garden.

66
00:02:07,904 --> 00:02:09,181
Maybe an orchard.

67
00:02:09,361 --> 00:02:11,847
The farm produced much of what the

68
00:02:11,847 --> 00:02:14,040
family ate, and sold the rest.

69
00:02:14,390 --> 00:02:16,956
Children helped from a very young age.

70
00:02:17,136 --> 00:02:20,188
A six-year-old gathered eggs and fed the

71
00:02:20,188 --> 00:02:20,875
chickens.

72
00:02:21,055 --> 00:02:23,901
A ten-year-old could drive the tractor in

73
00:02:23,901 --> 00:02:24,596
the field.

74
00:02:24,776 --> 00:02:27,295
Teenagers did the work of grown men.

75
00:02:27,475 --> 00:02:29,998
Schools in farm country often let out

76
00:02:29,998 --> 00:02:32,112
early in the spring and late in

77
00:02:32,112 --> 00:02:34,635
the fall, so children could help with

78
00:02:34,635 --> 00:02:36,066
planting and harvest.

79
00:02:36,416 --> 00:02:37,763
Every single day,

80
00:02:37,763 --> 00:02:39,426
no matter the season,

81
00:02:39,426 --> 00:02:40,851
there were chores.

82
00:02:41,031 --> 00:02:42,211
On a dairy farm,

83
00:02:42,211 --> 00:02:44,497
the cows had to be milked twice

84
00:02:44,497 --> 00:02:46,488
a day, at dawn and at dusk,

85
00:02:46,488 --> 00:02:49,217
three hundred sixty-five days a year.

86
00:02:49,397 --> 00:02:50,848
No vacations.

87
00:02:51,028 --> 00:02:52,409
No sick days.

88
00:02:52,589 --> 00:02:55,074
Christmas morning started in the barn,

89
00:02:55,074 --> 00:02:57,036
just like every other morning.

90
00:02:57,385 --> 00:02:58,629
By the fifties,

91
00:02:58,629 --> 00:03:01,281
many farms had milking machines,

92
00:03:01,281 --> 00:03:04,431
but plenty of small farms still milked

93
00:03:04,431 --> 00:03:05,094
by hand.

94
00:03:05,274 --> 00:03:07,689
A good milker could milk a cow

95
00:03:07,689 --> 00:03:10,023
in about five or ten minutes.

96
00:03:10,203 --> 00:03:12,465
The milk went into tall metal cans,

97
00:03:12,465 --> 00:03:14,405
which were cooled in a tank of

98
00:03:14,405 --> 00:03:16,279
cold water and picked up by a

99
00:03:16,279 --> 00:03:17,831
truck from the creamery.

100
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:19,937
There were pigs to feed,

101
00:03:19,937 --> 00:03:21,181
chickens to tend,

102
00:03:21,181 --> 00:03:22,278
eggs to gather,

103
00:03:22,278 --> 00:03:23,742
and stalls to clean.

104
00:03:23,922 --> 00:03:24,839
Water to pump.

105
00:03:25,019 --> 00:03:25,994
Wood to chop.

106
00:03:26,174 --> 00:03:27,335
Fences to mend.

107
00:03:27,515 --> 00:03:29,024
Machinery to repair.

108
00:03:29,204 --> 00:03:31,452
A farmer had to be a mechanic,

109
00:03:31,452 --> 00:03:33,551
a veterinarian, a carpenter,

110
00:03:33,551 --> 00:03:35,874
a weatherman and a businessman,

111
00:03:35,874 --> 00:03:37,447
all at the same time.

112
00:03:37,797 --> 00:03:40,081
The farm wife worked just as hard,

113
00:03:40,081 --> 00:03:41,222
and often harder.

114
00:03:41,402 --> 00:03:43,871
She cooked three big meals a day,

115
00:03:43,871 --> 00:03:46,564
from scratch, for her family and any

116
00:03:46,564 --> 00:03:47,312
hired men.

117
00:03:47,492 --> 00:03:49,018
She raised the children,

118
00:03:49,018 --> 00:03:49,971
kept the house,

119
00:03:49,971 --> 00:03:50,988
did the laundry,

120
00:03:50,988 --> 00:03:52,705
and tended the huge garden.

121
00:03:52,885 --> 00:03:54,278
She ran the chickens,

122
00:03:54,278 --> 00:03:56,601
and the money from selling eggs was

123
00:03:56,601 --> 00:03:57,862
often hers to keep,

124
00:03:57,862 --> 00:04:00,582
for things like shoes and school clothes.

125
00:04:00,932 --> 00:04:03,008
In the late summer and fall,

126
00:04:03,008 --> 00:04:03,823
she canned.

127
00:04:04,003 --> 00:04:06,553
Tomatoes, green beans,

128
00:04:06,553 --> 00:04:09,219
corn, peaches, pickles,

129
00:04:09,219 --> 00:04:11,190
jams and jellies.

130
00:04:11,370 --> 00:04:12,794
Hundreds of glass jars,

131
00:04:12,794 --> 00:04:14,899
lined up on shelves in the cellar.

132
00:04:15,079 --> 00:04:17,445
A good farm wife might can five

133
00:04:17,445 --> 00:04:19,735
hundred quarts or more a year.

134
00:04:19,915 --> 00:04:21,162
When winter came,

135
00:04:21,162 --> 00:04:24,315
that cellar was the family's grocery store.

136
00:04:24,665 --> 00:04:26,978
Farm wives also made many of the

137
00:04:26,978 --> 00:04:28,207
family's clothes.

138
00:04:28,387 --> 00:04:31,018
Feed and flour came in cotton sacks

139
00:04:31,018 --> 00:04:33,349
printed with colorful patterns,

140
00:04:33,349 --> 00:04:35,906
and women sewed them into dresses,

141
00:04:35,906 --> 00:04:38,011
shirts, aprons and curtains.

142
00:04:38,191 --> 00:04:41,101
Mills printed the sacks in pretty designs

143
00:04:41,101 --> 00:04:43,939
on purpose, because they knew farm women

144
00:04:43,939 --> 00:04:46,565
would choose the feed with the nicest

145
00:04:46,565 --> 00:04:47,061
fabric.

146
00:04:47,411 --> 00:04:50,148
The farmhouse itself was often old,

147
00:04:50,148 --> 00:04:53,588
built by a grandfather or great-grandfather.

148
00:04:53,768 --> 00:04:55,996
A big kitchen was the center of

149
00:04:55,996 --> 00:04:56,787
everything.

150
00:04:56,967 --> 00:05:00,600
Many farmhouses had only gotten electricity in

151
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:02,969
the last ten or fifteen years.

152
00:05:03,149 --> 00:05:04,896
In nineteen thirty-five,

153
00:05:04,896 --> 00:05:07,154
only about one in ten farms had

154
00:05:07,154 --> 00:05:08,246
electric power.

155
00:05:08,426 --> 00:05:10,237
By nineteen fifty-five,

156
00:05:10,237 --> 00:05:14,254
thanks to the Rural Electrification Administration,

157
00:05:14,254 --> 00:05:16,065
almost all of them did.

158
00:05:16,415 --> 00:05:19,819
Farm families remember the day the electricity

159
00:05:19,819 --> 00:05:20,189
came.

160
00:05:20,369 --> 00:05:22,592
Some of them stood in the kitchen

161
00:05:22,592 --> 00:05:24,882
and turned the light switch on and

162
00:05:24,882 --> 00:05:26,162
off, over and over.

163
00:05:26,342 --> 00:05:28,869
Electricity meant a refrigerator,

164
00:05:28,869 --> 00:05:30,248
a washing machine,

165
00:05:30,248 --> 00:05:33,004
a radio, an electric pump and lights

166
00:05:33,004 --> 00:05:33,923
in the barn.

167
00:05:34,103 --> 00:05:36,479
It changed farm life more than almost

168
00:05:36,479 --> 00:05:37,377
anything else.

169
00:05:37,727 --> 00:05:40,317
But indoor plumbing came more slowly.

170
00:05:40,497 --> 00:05:41,963
In nineteen fifty,

171
00:05:41,963 --> 00:05:45,628
many farmhouses still had no indoor bathroom.

172
00:05:45,808 --> 00:05:47,779
Families used an outhouse,

173
00:05:47,779 --> 00:05:49,826
and took baths in a tin tub

174
00:05:49,826 --> 00:05:52,328
in the kitchen on Saturday night,

175
00:05:52,328 --> 00:05:54,678
with water heated on the stove.

176
00:05:54,858 --> 00:05:56,587
By the end of the fifties,

177
00:05:56,587 --> 00:05:58,648
most farms had finally put in a

178
00:05:58,648 --> 00:05:59,247
bathroom.

179
00:05:59,596 --> 00:06:01,845
Many farms still had a party line

180
00:06:01,845 --> 00:06:04,912
telephone, shared with six or eight neighbors

181
00:06:04,912 --> 00:06:05,866
down the road.

182
00:06:06,046 --> 00:06:07,985
Each house had its own ring.

183
00:06:08,165 --> 00:06:10,217
Everyone knew everyone's business,

184
00:06:10,217 --> 00:06:12,089
because anyone could listen in.

185
00:06:12,269 --> 00:06:14,254
When there was an emergency,

186
00:06:14,254 --> 00:06:16,168
like a fire or a bad storm,

187
00:06:16,168 --> 00:06:18,862
the operator could ring every house on

188
00:06:18,862 --> 00:06:20,067
the line at once,

189
00:06:20,067 --> 00:06:22,265
and the neighbors came running.

190
00:06:22,615 --> 00:06:23,974
Summer meant haying,

191
00:06:23,974 --> 00:06:25,332
and haying was hard.

192
00:06:25,512 --> 00:06:26,786
The hay was cut,

193
00:06:26,786 --> 00:06:28,617
left to dry in the sun,

194
00:06:28,617 --> 00:06:29,891
raked into rows,

195
00:06:29,891 --> 00:06:31,085
and then baled.

196
00:06:31,265 --> 00:06:34,072
Each bale weighed fifty or sixty pounds,

197
00:06:34,072 --> 00:06:36,037
and had to be lifted by hand

198
00:06:36,037 --> 00:06:36,949
onto a wagon,

199
00:06:36,949 --> 00:06:39,195
then thrown up into the hayloft.

200
00:06:39,375 --> 00:06:42,138
Teenage boys bucked hay all summer and

201
00:06:42,138 --> 00:06:44,320
came out of it strong as oxen.

202
00:06:44,670 --> 00:06:47,074
The race was always against the rain.

203
00:06:47,254 --> 00:06:48,623
Wet hay could rot,

204
00:06:48,623 --> 00:06:50,981
or even catch fire in the barn.

205
00:06:51,161 --> 00:06:53,031
So when the hay was ready,

206
00:06:53,031 --> 00:06:55,909
everybody worked, from sunup until dark.

207
00:06:56,089 --> 00:06:58,464
The kitchen sent out jugs of cold

208
00:06:58,464 --> 00:07:00,552
water, lemonade and iced tea,

209
00:07:00,552 --> 00:07:03,287
and sandwiches wrapped in dish towels.

210
00:07:03,637 --> 00:07:05,764
And summer evenings on the farm were

211
00:07:05,764 --> 00:07:06,354
beautiful.

212
00:07:06,534 --> 00:07:08,800
After supper, the family sat on the

213
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:11,000
porch while the sun went down over

214
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:11,712
the fields.

215
00:07:11,892 --> 00:07:13,715
Fireflies came out.

216
00:07:13,895 --> 00:07:15,451
Somewhere a cow called.

217
00:07:15,631 --> 00:07:17,725
The radio played a ball game or

218
00:07:17,725 --> 00:07:19,009
the Grand Ole Opry.

219
00:07:19,189 --> 00:07:21,384
And everyone went to bed early,

220
00:07:21,384 --> 00:07:23,578
because morning came very soon.

221
00:07:23,927 --> 00:07:24,970
Fall was harvest,

222
00:07:24,970 --> 00:07:27,178
the most important time of the year.

223
00:07:27,358 --> 00:07:30,047
Corn was picked with a mechanical corn

224
00:07:30,047 --> 00:07:32,524
picker, and the ears were stored in

225
00:07:32,524 --> 00:07:33,303
a corncrib.

226
00:07:33,483 --> 00:07:36,339
Oats and wheat were cut and threshed.

227
00:07:36,519 --> 00:07:37,631
In earlier years,

228
00:07:37,631 --> 00:07:40,052
neighbors went from farm to farm with

229
00:07:40,052 --> 00:07:41,819
a shared threshing machine,

230
00:07:41,819 --> 00:07:44,437
and the whole community worked together.

231
00:07:44,786 --> 00:07:47,449
Those threshing days were legendary for the

232
00:07:47,449 --> 00:07:47,759
food.

233
00:07:47,939 --> 00:07:50,752
The farm wives cooked enormous dinners for

234
00:07:50,752 --> 00:07:51,422
the crews.

235
00:07:51,602 --> 00:07:54,299
Fried chicken, roast beef,

236
00:07:54,299 --> 00:07:56,685
mashed potatoes, gravy,

237
00:07:56,685 --> 00:07:58,552
fresh bread, corn,

238
00:07:58,552 --> 00:08:02,079
green beans and four or five kinds

239
00:08:02,079 --> 00:08:02,805
of pie.

240
00:08:02,985 --> 00:08:05,103
The men ate at long tables in

241
00:08:05,103 --> 00:08:07,658
the yard, and the women competed to

242
00:08:07,658 --> 00:08:09,046
set the best table.

243
00:08:09,395 --> 00:08:11,462
Fall also meant butchering.

244
00:08:11,642 --> 00:08:13,437
On a cold day in late fall,

245
00:08:13,437 --> 00:08:15,765
families butchered a hog or a steer

246
00:08:15,765 --> 00:08:16,762
for the winter.

247
00:08:16,942 --> 00:08:18,417
Neighbors came to help.

248
00:08:18,597 --> 00:08:19,908
Nothing was wasted.

249
00:08:20,088 --> 00:08:21,823
They made sausage,

250
00:08:21,823 --> 00:08:24,907
bacon, ham, lard and headcheese.

251
00:08:25,087 --> 00:08:27,143
Hams were cured and hung in the

252
00:08:27,143 --> 00:08:27,873
smokehouse.

253
00:08:28,053 --> 00:08:28,894
It was hard,

254
00:08:28,894 --> 00:08:31,416
messy work, and an important part of

255
00:08:31,416 --> 00:08:32,047
the year.

256
00:08:32,396 --> 00:08:33,940
Winter was slower,

257
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:35,055
but not easy.

258
00:08:35,235 --> 00:08:37,206
The cows still had to be milked,

259
00:08:37,206 --> 00:08:39,115
and the animals still had to be

260
00:08:39,115 --> 00:08:40,901
fed, in snow and bitter cold.

261
00:08:41,081 --> 00:08:43,476
Water froze in the troughs and had

262
00:08:43,476 --> 00:08:45,237
to be broken with an axe.

263
00:08:45,417 --> 00:08:48,147
Farmers repaired their machinery in the barn,

264
00:08:48,147 --> 00:08:49,724
and planned for next year.

265
00:08:50,074 --> 00:08:52,420
Winter was also the time for visiting.

266
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:55,242
Farm families went to church suppers,

267
00:08:55,242 --> 00:08:57,955
card parties, and dances at the grange

268
00:08:57,955 --> 00:08:58,312
hall.

269
00:08:58,492 --> 00:09:00,791
Women held quilting bees.

270
00:09:00,971 --> 00:09:03,090
Men gathered at the feed store or

271
00:09:03,090 --> 00:09:05,273
the co-op to talk about prices and

272
00:09:05,273 --> 00:09:06,044
the weather.

273
00:09:06,224 --> 00:09:08,856
Children went sledding and skating on the

274
00:09:08,856 --> 00:09:09,626
frozen pond.

275
00:09:09,975 --> 00:09:13,604
Many farm children in nineteen fifty-five still

276
00:09:13,604 --> 00:09:15,920
went to one-room schoolhouses.

277
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:18,823
One teacher taught all eight grades in

278
00:09:18,823 --> 00:09:19,827
a single room,

279
00:09:19,827 --> 00:09:21,475
heated by a wood stove.

280
00:09:21,655 --> 00:09:24,256
The older children helped the younger ones.

281
00:09:24,436 --> 00:09:25,696
In nineteen thirty,

282
00:09:25,696 --> 00:09:28,216
there were about one hundred and fifty

283
00:09:28,216 --> 00:09:30,670
thousand one-room schools in America.

284
00:09:30,850 --> 00:09:32,297
By the late fifties,

285
00:09:32,297 --> 00:09:33,598
most were closing,

286
00:09:33,598 --> 00:09:36,780
as country schools were combined into bigger

287
00:09:36,780 --> 00:09:37,503
districts.

288
00:09:37,852 --> 00:09:39,454
Farm kids joined 4-H,

289
00:09:39,454 --> 00:09:41,361
where they raised calves,

290
00:09:41,361 --> 00:09:43,268
pigs, lambs and chickens,

291
00:09:43,268 --> 00:09:45,481
sewed dresses and baked pies,

292
00:09:45,481 --> 00:09:48,151
and showed them at the county fair.

293
00:09:48,331 --> 00:09:50,072
The fair was the high point of

294
00:09:50,072 --> 00:09:50,711
the summer.

295
00:09:50,891 --> 00:09:52,967
A blue ribbon at the county fair

296
00:09:52,967 --> 00:09:55,174
was a matter of enormous pride for

297
00:09:55,174 --> 00:09:56,278
the whole family.

298
00:09:56,627 --> 00:09:58,653
And the whole community leaned on each

299
00:09:58,653 --> 00:09:58,973
other.

300
00:09:59,153 --> 00:10:00,807
When a barn burned down,

301
00:10:00,807 --> 00:10:03,425
neighbors gathered to raise a new one.

302
00:10:03,605 --> 00:10:05,811
When a farmer was hurt or sick,

303
00:10:05,811 --> 00:10:08,586
neighbors harvested his crops for free.

304
00:10:08,766 --> 00:10:10,380
Nobody had to ask.

305
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:13,182
It was simply understood that farm families

306
00:10:13,182 --> 00:10:14,646
took care of each other.

307
00:10:14,996 --> 00:10:17,446
So why did this world vanish?

308
00:10:17,626 --> 00:10:20,516
The answer is that farming changed faster

309
00:10:20,516 --> 00:10:23,547
than almost anything else in American life.

310
00:10:23,727 --> 00:10:26,730
Bigger tractors and machines could farm far

311
00:10:26,730 --> 00:10:28,685
more land with fewer people.

312
00:10:28,865 --> 00:10:31,079
A single farmer could do the work

313
00:10:31,079 --> 00:10:31,616
of five.

314
00:10:31,796 --> 00:10:33,901
And it took more and more money

315
00:10:33,901 --> 00:10:35,395
to buy those machines.

316
00:10:35,745 --> 00:10:38,283
Prices for crops didn't keep up with

317
00:10:38,283 --> 00:10:38,706
costs.

318
00:10:38,886 --> 00:10:41,714
Farmers kept hearing the same hard advice

319
00:10:41,714 --> 00:10:43,576
from experts and officials.

320
00:10:43,756 --> 00:10:45,695
Get big, or get out.

321
00:10:45,875 --> 00:10:48,711
Many small farmers sold to their neighbors,

322
00:10:48,711 --> 00:10:51,019
and moved to town for factory jobs.

323
00:10:51,199 --> 00:10:53,827
Their children went to college and never

324
00:10:53,827 --> 00:10:54,484
came back.

325
00:10:54,664 --> 00:10:57,913
Between nineteen fifty and nineteen seventy,

326
00:10:57,913 --> 00:11:00,424
the number of farms in America was

327
00:11:00,424 --> 00:11:01,827
cut nearly in half.

328
00:11:02,177 --> 00:11:04,363
Then came the farm crisis of the

329
00:11:04,363 --> 00:11:07,572
nineteen eighties, when high interest rates and

330
00:11:07,572 --> 00:11:10,509
falling land values put thousands of family

331
00:11:10,509 --> 00:11:12,011
farms out of business.

332
00:11:12,191 --> 00:11:15,349
Auctions were held all over the Midwest.

333
00:11:15,529 --> 00:11:18,161
Farmers watched their tractors and their family

334
00:11:18,161 --> 00:11:19,952
land sold to the highest bidder.

335
00:11:20,132 --> 00:11:22,350
It was one of the saddest chapters

336
00:11:22,350 --> 00:11:24,045
in rural American history.

337
00:11:24,394 --> 00:11:26,973
Today, American farms are bigger,

338
00:11:26,973 --> 00:11:30,490
more efficient and more productive than ever.

339
00:11:30,670 --> 00:11:33,913
But the family farm of nineteen fifty-five,

340
00:11:33,913 --> 00:11:35,346
with its milk cows,

341
00:11:35,346 --> 00:11:38,061
chickens, garden and canning cellar,

342
00:11:38,061 --> 00:11:40,399
has almost completely vanished.

343
00:11:40,579 --> 00:11:43,180
Many old farmhouses stand empty.

344
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:46,587
Barns are falling down along country roads.

345
00:11:46,937 --> 00:11:48,841
What remains is the memory.

346
00:11:49,021 --> 00:11:50,856
The smell of fresh-cut hay.

347
00:11:51,036 --> 00:11:53,786
The sound of a screen door slamming

348
00:11:53,786 --> 00:11:54,414
in July.

349
00:11:54,594 --> 00:11:57,251
The taste of fresh milk and homemade

350
00:11:57,251 --> 00:11:57,694
bread.

351
00:11:57,874 --> 00:12:00,253
The feeling of a whole community working

352
00:12:00,253 --> 00:12:00,788
together.

353
00:12:00,968 --> 00:12:02,902
For people who grew up on a

354
00:12:02,902 --> 00:12:05,194
farm, those memories never fade.

355
00:12:05,544 --> 00:12:07,309
And the lessons remain too.

356
00:12:07,489 --> 00:12:08,406
Hard work.

357
00:12:08,586 --> 00:12:09,863
Self-reliance.

358
00:12:10,043 --> 00:12:12,040
Taking care of your neighbors.

359
00:12:12,220 --> 00:12:13,683
Not wasting anything.

360
00:12:13,863 --> 00:12:16,545
Generations of Americans who left the farm

361
00:12:16,545 --> 00:12:18,972
carried those lessons into the cities,

362
00:12:18,972 --> 00:12:20,887
the factories and the offices,

363
00:12:20,887 --> 00:12:22,867
and passed them on to their own

364
00:12:22,867 --> 00:12:23,441
children.

365
00:12:23,791 --> 00:12:25,742
So tell us in the comments.

366
00:12:25,922 --> 00:12:27,622
Did you grow up on a farm,

367
00:12:27,622 --> 00:12:29,846
or visit grandparents who had one?

368
00:12:30,026 --> 00:12:31,684
What chore did you hate the most,

369
00:12:31,684 --> 00:12:32,789
and what did you love?

370
00:12:32,969 --> 00:12:34,293
We read every comment.

371
00:12:34,473 --> 00:12:36,679
Next, we're looking at what it was

372
00:12:36,679 --> 00:12:39,535
like to start college in nineteen sixty-six.

373
00:12:39,715 --> 00:12:41,637
Thank you for spending this time with

374
00:12:41,637 --> 00:12:41,793
me.
